# L4-C3 — sharpness of the ratio hypothesis: the exact failing input

`Poincare.L4.DoublingToCovers.Counterexample` shows that uniform local doubling plus a
uniform diameter bound does not imply the uniform-cover hypothesis of the D12 interface.
`Poincare.L4.DoublingToCovers.Consumption` isolates the extra hypotheses that *do* suffice:
uniform ball-ratio constants and uniform non-collapse at the covering scale
(`uniformCovers_of_ratio_data`).

This file closes the loop between the two: it proves that on the discrete counterexample
family the ratio hypothesis of `uniformCovers_of_ratio_data` fails, quantitatively.  On the
`n`-point discrete space with counting measure the ratio data at scale `ε = 1/4` force
`n ≤ C₁`, because the large ball `closedBall y (2D)` is the whole space while the small ball
`closedBall y (1/8)` is the singleton `{y}`, so the ratio data says
`μ univ ≤ C₁ * μ {y}` for *every* point `y`; summing over the `n` points gives
`n * μ univ ≤ C₁ * μ univ`, and cancellation (non-collapse + finiteness at the point scale)
gives `n ≤ C₁`.

Consequences:

* `Disc.card_le_of_uniform_ratio` — the quantitative sharpness lemma;
* `not_uniformRatioData_discreteFamily` — no choice of constants satisfies the ratio data of
  `uniformCovers_of_ratio_data` uniformly on the discrete family (at `ε = 1/4`, any `D ≥ 1`);
* `uniformCovers_of_ratio_data_hypothesis_fails` — the full hypothesis bundle of
  `uniformCovers_of_ratio_data` is *false* for the discrete family;
* `exists_doubling_coveringNumber_gt` — for every `M` there is a compact metric space of
  diameter at most `1` carrying a uniformly locally doubling measure whose covering number at
  any radius `< 1` exceeds `M`: no bound on covering numbers follows from uniform local
  doubling and a diameter bound alone.
-/
